    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Plenty of bedrooms, especially in older houses and rented flats, have no ceiling light at all. Instead there is a switched socket by the door, or nothing but a couple of wall sockets. It can feel like a problem, but a bedroom lit entirely by lamps is often nicer than one lit by a single central bulb. The light sits lower, the shadows are softer and you can set the mood for reading, dressing or winding down.</p>
<p>The trick is planning. A few lamps dotted around at random leave dark corners and trailing cables. A deliberate layout, with each light doing a specific job, gives you a room that is easy to use from the moment you walk in. Here is how to plan a no overhead light bedroom lamp layout that works for renters and homeowners alike.</p>

<h2 id="start-with-the-switch">Start with the door: solve the "walking into a dark room" problem</h2>
<p>The biggest frustration in a room without a ceiling light is fumbling across a dark floor to find a lamp switch. Fix this first.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Use a switched socket if you have one.</strong> Many UK bedrooms without a ceiling light have a wall switch by the door that controls one socket. Plug your main floor lamp into that socket and the switch works just like a ceiling light switch would.</li>
<li><strong>Smart plugs.</strong> A smart plug on your main lamp lets you switch it on from your phone, a voice assistant or a schedule. Some come with a battery-powered wireless wall switch you can stick by the door, which is the closest thing to a real light switch without rewiring.</li>
<li><strong>Remote-control plug sockets.</strong> A simpler, non-app option: a set of plug-in sockets with a small remote control. Keep the remote on a hook by the door.</li>
</ul>
<p>Whichever you choose, the lamp nearest the door should be bright enough to cross the room safely, so it is usually your floor lamp rather than a small bedside light.</p>

<h2 id="the-four-jobs">The four jobs your lamps need to do</h2>
<p>Instead of thinking "how many lamps", think about the jobs that a ceiling light would normally do, and give each one to a specific light.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>General light:</strong> enough to see the whole room, put clothes away and find things. A floor lamp with an upward or diffused shade, or a swag pendant, does this best.</li>
<li><strong>Task light:</strong> focused light for reading in bed, dressing and doing make-up. Bedside lamps, plug-in wall sconces and a mirror light cover this.</li>
<li><strong>Ambient or accent light:</strong> a soft glow for the evening, such as an LED strip behind the headboard or a small lamp on a chest of drawers.</li>
<li><strong>Night light:</strong> a very low light for getting up in the night without waking yourself fully, such as a motion-sensor strip under the bed.</li>
</ul>
<p>For more on how these layers work together, see our <a href="/blog/layered-lighting-room-by-room-guide">layered lighting guide</a>.</p>

<h2 id="sample-layout">A sample lamp layout for a typical double bedroom</h2>
<p>Take a typical double bedroom of around 3.3 × 3.6 m (11 × 12 ft) with a double bed against the wall opposite the window, a wardrobe on one side wall and a chest of drawers on the other. Here is a layout that covers every job.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Floor lamp in the corner nearest the door.</strong> Choose an uplighter or a lamp with a large fabric drum shade, around 150–180 cm (59–71 in) tall. Bouncing light off the ceiling and walls gives the most even general light. Plug it into the switched socket or a smart plug.</li>
<li><strong>Two bedside lights.</strong> Either table lamps or plug-in wall sconces. For table lamps, the bottom of the shade should sit roughly at your chin height when you are sitting up in bed, usually around 50–60 cm (20–24 in) above the mattress top. Wall sconces are typically mounted so the light source is around 100–120 cm (39–47 in) above the floor, but check against your own bed height.</li>
<li><strong>A small lamp on the chest of drawers.</strong> This fills the opposite side of the room so it never feels lopsided, and it doubles as a soft evening light.</li>
<li><strong>An LED strip behind the headboard (optional).</strong> A warm-white strip on the back of the headboard gives a gentle wash up the wall.</li>
<li><strong>A motion-sensor night light (optional).</strong> A battery strip under the bed frame or along the skirting on your side of the bed.</li>
</ul>
<p>That gives four to five light sources at different heights: high (floor lamp), middle (bedside lights) and low (strip and night light). The variety of heights is what makes a lamp-lit room feel balanced rather than gloomy.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before you buy anything, sketch the room on paper and mark every socket. Then place each lamp within reach of a socket so you need as few extension leads as possible.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="renter-friendly-options">Renter-friendly lighting options</h2>
<h3>Plug-in wall sconces</h3>
<p>Plug-in sconces have a cable and plug instead of being wired into the wall, so you only need one or two small screw holes to hang them. That is far less damage than chasing in a cable. Many renters fill and touch up small screw holes before they leave, but check your tenancy agreement and ask your landlord in writing if you are unsure. Look for sconces with an inline switch or a dimmer on the cable, and use a cable cover painted to match the wall for a neater finish.</p>
<p>If you cannot drill at all, rechargeable battery wall lights that fix with a magnetic plate or adhesive pad are an alternative. Test any adhesive on a hidden spot first, and follow the manufacturer's weight limits.</p>

<!--figure:bedroom-lit-headboard|A lit upholstered headboard gives this bedroom a soft background glow alongside sheer curtains and a pendant light.-->
<h3>Swag pendants on ceiling hooks</h3>
<p>A swag pendant is a ceiling light with a long fabric cable and a plug. The cable is looped from a hook above the bed or in the centre of the ceiling, across to a second hook near the wall, and down to a socket. You get the look of a ceiling light without any wiring. A pendant hanging over a bedside table, around 40–60 cm (16–24 in) above the table top, also makes a stylish alternative to a table lamp.</p>
<p>Ceiling hooks usually need a small hole, so they are worth asking your landlord about. Adhesive hooks are not suitable for holding a light fitting over a bed. Choose a lightweight shade, such as rattan, paper or fabric, and fix the hook into a joist or with fixings suitable for your ceiling type.</p>

<h3>Floor lamps</h3>
<p>The workhorse of a room without a ceiling light. Uplighters give the broadest general light. Arc floor lamps can reach over a reading chair or the side of a bed. A tripod lamp with a fabric shade adds style and a soft glow. In a small bedroom, a slim floor lamp tucked beside the wardrobe uses no surface space at all.</p>

<h3>LED strips</h3>
<p>Plug-in LED strips are inexpensive and versatile. Use them behind a headboard, along the top of a wardrobe to bounce light off the ceiling, or under a bed frame as a night light. Most stick on with adhesive backing, which can lift paint when removed, so apply them to furniture rather than walls where you can.</p>

<h2 id="colour-temperature">Choose the right bulbs: colour temperature and brightness</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Colour temperature:</strong> for bedrooms, choose warm white bulbs of 2700–3000K. 2700K feels cosy and golden; 3000K is a little crisper and useful for a dressing area. Avoid cool white (4000K and above) in a bedroom, as it tends to feel clinical in the evening.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it consistent:</strong> use the same colour temperature in every lamp in the room. Mixing 2700K and 4000K bulbs makes the room look patchy.</li>
<li><strong>Dimmable where possible:</strong> plug-in dimmers, dimmable bulbs or smart bulbs let one lamp go from bright to soft. Check that the bulb and the dimmer are compatible.</li>
<li><strong>Brightness:</strong> your main floor lamp needs to be the brightest light in the room. Bedside lamps can be lower, as they only need to light a book.</li>
<li><strong>Shades matter:</strong> fabric and paper shades soften light; opal glass diffuses it evenly; open bulbs can glare. Always stay within the maximum wattage marked on the lamp.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="cable-management">Cable management and safety</h2>
<p>Lots of lamps means lots of cables. Handled carelessly, they look messy and can be a trip or overheating hazard.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do not overload extension leads.</strong> Check the maximum rating marked on the lead and add up what you plug into it. LED lamps draw very little, but heaters, hair dryers and chargers sharing the same lead add up quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Do not daisy-chain</strong> extension leads by plugging one into another. If you need more sockets, use a single extension lead of the right length, ideally one with surge protection and individual switches.</li>
<li><strong>Keep cables out of walkways.</strong> Run them along the skirting and behind furniture rather than across the floor, and never under rugs, where damage can go unnoticed.</li>
<li><strong>Tidy what you can see.</strong> Self-adhesive cable clips, paintable cable covers and fabric cable sleeves keep runs neat. A cable tidy box hides an extension lead and its plugs under the bed or behind a bedside table.</li>
<li><strong>Do not cover lamps or strips</strong> with fabric, and keep bulbs away from curtains and bedding.</li>
<li><strong>Check old lamps.</strong> Replace any with frayed cables or cracked plugs, and if in doubt ask a qualified electrician.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="curtains-and-colour">Let curtains and colour help</h2>
<p>A lamp-lit room relies on reflected light, so the surfaces matter. Pale or warm wall colours bounce light around; very dark walls absorb it, which can be lovely and cocooning but means you will need more lamps. A mirror opposite a lamp doubles its effect. Lined curtains in a mid-tone keep the evening glow in and the streetlight out.</p>

